WEBVTT

00:09.809 --> 00:17.357
I don't know how much Sean has told you.

00:17.397 --> 00:17.938
Not very much.

00:18.519 --> 00:27.389
He was surprised in some cases, but interested in my background.

00:30.975 --> 00:35.536
And said, well, I know somebody who would like to talk to you.

00:35.696 --> 00:40.577
And then you mentioned in your email what your function is, so.

00:40.617 --> 00:41.078
Yeah.

00:42.918 --> 00:45.959
I'm just, I'd like to do background on people I'm interviewing.

00:45.979 --> 00:46.959
So I did a little digging.

00:47.539 --> 00:50.020
And just to see that you worked here in the 90s, is that right?

00:51.988 --> 00:54.209
Did you work here in the 90s?

00:54.249 --> 00:56.629
Is that what the time was?

00:56.649 --> 01:03.051
Yeah, I came in 1985 and retired the first one in 2000.

01:03.811 --> 01:05.291
Then they called me back.

01:05.471 --> 01:08.952
You know, it's like bad cars, they get recalled.

01:10.493 --> 01:12.513
And so I retired again in 2002 and again in 2003.

01:12.573 --> 01:13.554
You can't stay away.

01:19.735 --> 01:27.081
Well, my replacement kept either quitting or not coming.

01:29.583 --> 01:46.417
And at that time, in fact, that's what I came for, is they had the beginning of a separate telecommunication program, separate from electronic, the common base for, I guess,

01:48.899 --> 01:53.102
Yeah, freshman and sophomore, and then they branch out into telecom.

01:53.603 --> 01:58.086
And my role was to develop the telecom part.

02:04.645 --> 02:13.428
Yeah, so when they did call me back, I said, well, I'm retired.

02:13.448 --> 02:20.950
They said, yeah, but you've got juniors that can't graduate because there's nobody offered a course.

02:20.970 --> 02:21.070
Yeah.

02:22.550 --> 02:23.291
Okay, okay.

02:23.371 --> 02:24.851
That's a tricky situation.

02:24.911 --> 02:26.812
It's like they got you there.

02:27.553 --> 02:29.273
They know which button to push.

02:30.934 --> 02:36.157
I wasn't forced to accept.

02:36.677 --> 02:36.917
Right.

02:39.078 --> 02:40.419
So I'm going to be honest.

02:40.519 --> 02:42.700
I know about technology.

02:42.740 --> 02:44.821
I'm not very technologically savvy.

02:45.241 --> 02:48.743
So I was doing a little background research too into your field.

02:50.079 --> 02:53.744
It very quickly turned into another foreign language to me.

02:53.764 --> 03:00.334
But it was so interesting seeing how you were part of the

03:02.358 --> 03:07.544
great technological shift on the university, like you were part of that, you were there, you saw that.

03:07.644 --> 03:17.755
Yeah, well, that period of time, telecom was booming, and the technology was also moving very fast.

03:18.816 --> 03:22.640
But we actually got after the...

03:23.621 --> 03:33.404
second, third year, a separate accreditation for the telecom program, ABET accreditation, so that was good.

03:33.624 --> 03:35.004
Yeah, absolutely.

03:35.584 --> 03:46.847
We were, I think, the second strictly telecom program as opposed to electronic, electricity.

03:48.810 --> 03:52.193
and the first undergraduate to get accredited.

03:52.653 --> 03:53.974
So that was good.

03:54.154 --> 03:55.875
Yeah, absolutely.

03:56.176 --> 04:01.159
Sorry that I'm jumping right into this, but how was that process of getting accredited?

04:01.359 --> 04:03.221
What did that entail?

04:03.641 --> 04:05.963
Oh, that's a very long process.

04:05.983 --> 04:08.585
There's this much paperwork.

04:13.790 --> 04:22.510
They want to know all the description of the program, how many people, students do you have, how many graduates, how many have

04:23.532 --> 04:25.614
are employed, et cetera, et cetera.

04:26.054 --> 04:30.558
But each course, again, a detail, not just a summary.

04:30.598 --> 04:33.040
They want to see everything on the course.

04:33.921 --> 04:37.104
It's a very long preparation process.

04:37.684 --> 04:42.388
And everybody has to do it, but...

04:44.390 --> 04:50.395
Does that go to like, whenever you finish that, does that go to like a national type of?

04:50.435 --> 04:50.976
Yeah, yes.

04:51.756 --> 05:12.252
And so you submit that and so professors of other universities that have a telecom program or something related or are professionally involved in telecom review all of these and then decided, yeah, let's go visit or not.

05:14.054 --> 05:25.404
And so there's a group of three or four people that come and spend two, three days here looking at the labs, looking at the, talking, interviewing the students.

05:27.246 --> 05:30.849
It's a good process, but it takes a lot of time.

05:30.869 --> 05:32.991
Yeah, I can definitely see that.

05:34.492 --> 05:37.975
I would love to get you in touch with this gentleman named Mark Baggett.

05:38.756 --> 05:39.257
He does,

05:39.957 --> 05:45.503
the technological services for the library, but he has access to the supercomputers that we have here.

05:45.523 --> 05:51.288
So I was like, I feel like that's something... Well, that's a different orientation, but...

05:56.457 --> 06:01.700
Can you tell me about where you were born and where you were raised?

06:02.300 --> 06:09.364
I was born in Paris, France, just before World War II in 1937.

06:14.106 --> 06:35.072
We had to, because of the German army invasion of France, we had to move, I think, three or four times during that period to, well, it's...

06:36.883 --> 06:50.056
I was just a kid, I was a little kid, so, but I do remember, suddenly, no, we gotta go, and it was not like next month, it's tomorrow.

06:53.680 --> 06:55.061
Who was in your family at that time?

06:56.543 --> 07:06.233
Well, my kids, I was an only child, but my...

07:11.447 --> 07:29.355
Let's see, yeah, my biological father had to leave because of the war situation and mother was left alone with me.

07:30.436 --> 07:38.819
But later on, I guess, I don't know the details,

07:40.902 --> 07:48.638
My parents divorced, and again, I know he had gone overseas.

07:50.935 --> 07:52.156
And I don't know the details.

07:52.216 --> 07:53.837
Mother didn't want to talk about it.

07:55.338 --> 07:56.078
Right.

07:56.098 --> 07:58.740
But she remarried.

07:59.880 --> 08:12.688
And that, amazingly, that new father of mine, I mean, literally adopted me.

08:14.028 --> 08:19.191
And we went through the war and...

08:21.353 --> 08:27.597
And he took a chance because he was,

08:31.181 --> 08:36.845
I don't remember which level, co-director of a factory.

08:38.326 --> 08:44.390
And of course, Germans took that over and so we left again.

08:46.491 --> 08:53.636
So it was, and all the bombardments and this and that.

08:54.716 --> 08:56.938
Can I ask his name and your mom's name?

08:58.635 --> 09:02.937
I don't know if they want that.

09:04.959 --> 09:09.742
Yeah, I think I'll pass on that.

09:09.762 --> 09:10.082
Sorry.

09:11.824 --> 09:18.068
Well, actually, my biological father, you can have his first name was René.

09:19.817 --> 09:34.405
He was a, he had a, from what I read much later on, he had a PhD in marine biology.

09:37.306 --> 09:58.991
And in fact, went to, that was after the war was over, went to New Caledonia, which is a small island in the Pacific, north of New Zealand, way north, and set up an aquarium there.

09:59.031 --> 10:04.272
He founded the aquarium and became quite famous in his field.

10:04.812 --> 10:05.332
Wow.

10:06.533 --> 10:11.617
It's three, we published three big books.

10:13.479 --> 10:22.708
So, but I actually, no, he left, that was a year and a half, I think.

10:24.150 --> 10:25.111
And I never met him.

10:27.065 --> 10:31.068
Well, that's on the other side of the world, literally.

10:31.128 --> 10:33.430
Right, yeah.

10:34.310 --> 10:40.415
Well, we stayed in touch, writing letters and things.

10:44.099 --> 10:47.042
But your mom, how strong, how strong, wow.

10:47.162 --> 10:52.526
Yeah, she had to, she had to face lots of other problems.

10:53.507 --> 11:02.075
Well, single mother and, you know, the financial input that disappeared, so she had to find a job.

11:04.116 --> 11:09.941
And yeah, and she has her own Ph.D. in sociology.

11:10.162 --> 11:10.522
Wow.

11:11.643 --> 11:17.008
So I had two imposing parents.

11:17.028 --> 11:19.490
Yeah, very educated.

11:20.971 --> 11:21.952
How did you end up...

11:24.090 --> 11:28.154
That was a long circuitous thing.

11:28.334 --> 11:38.122
I studied in France for high school, of course, and then it's like a junior college, but it's specialized.

11:38.462 --> 11:48.891
It was strictly radio and electronics, except for some civics type things.

11:50.672 --> 11:59.980
And then I did my military service in France for three years in their equivalent of the Signal Corps.

12:00.060 --> 12:02.342
So I was radio again.

12:07.786 --> 12:13.591
Since I had done two years of radio school, that made sense for them to put me there.

12:15.733 --> 12:21.698
And after that, I worked at Paris for a year and a half.

12:24.233 --> 12:35.323
And then a friend of mine, who I met in France several times, said, I had mentioned it, he said, you know, you need to come and visit us.

12:35.423 --> 12:39.747
I said, well, yeah, but financial, I think, plus.

12:40.628 --> 12:48.135
He said, well, and then he actually got me a work permit for a year.

12:48.195 --> 12:50.077
So I went there, and the idea was,

12:50.357 --> 13:18.441
well I'll work and I'll save as much money as possible and then I'll play tourist and but the it's not the case anymore but we're talking 1964-65 the telecom technology in the US was easily 10 years ahead of the French one

13:20.761 --> 13:22.662
the telephone network at that time.

13:22.682 --> 13:29.023
You know, there was no internet and digital transmission at that time, but telephone network in France.

13:29.423 --> 13:37.305
It was not a priority, and the telecom was government-owned, like most of Europe.

13:37.605 --> 13:42.046
Yeah, almost, yeah, actually, all of Europe.

13:44.067 --> 13:48.688
The postal service and the telecommunications were government-owned.

13:50.649 --> 14:12.303
And the telephone network, I mean, this is not, it's what, 10 years after the end of World War II, they had a lot of other problems and their decision was, yeah, we have a telephone network, we'll modernize it later, we got to use the money.

14:13.223 --> 14:15.686
for other things, yeah.

14:15.806 --> 14:22.415
So that, I discovered that very quickly, that wow, this is fascinating.

14:23.556 --> 14:26.540
And they, they,

14:31.665 --> 14:36.547
I'm a ham radio operator, so I met some American ham.

14:36.587 --> 14:38.868
I was in New Hampshire for that work.

14:40.649 --> 14:48.192
And one of them, actually, well,

14:51.713 --> 14:55.874
I told him that my work permit was going to expire.

14:56.954 --> 15:04.076
And he said, well, you know, hold on.

15:04.776 --> 15:13.978
He had a small repair and installation shop for mobile radios.

15:15.038 --> 15:23.141
and he was specialized in equipping and maintaining radios for fire departments.

15:23.561 --> 15:28.003
Most of them volunteer fire departments around the south of New Hampshire.

15:29.063 --> 15:39.287
And he said, I had the technician who messed up seriously almost six months ago, and I desperately need somebody.

15:39.347 --> 15:40.908
He said, would you be interested?

15:41.508 --> 15:41.728
Yep.

15:42.869 --> 15:44.191
Well, let's see what I can do.

15:44.551 --> 15:55.941
And so I guess he convinced the immigration folks that I really need somebody and I haven't been able to find a small town in New Hampshire.

15:57.022 --> 15:59.724
And so I got another work permit.

16:01.386 --> 16:10.453
And while I was doing that, I met a young lady who is now my wife, and we've been married for 57 years, I think.

16:11.514 --> 16:14.436
So made the right decision, obviously.

16:15.977 --> 16:23.523
So I worked there, and then same thing, you know, I've got a one-year period.

16:24.404 --> 16:28.787
So I went to immigration in Boston.

16:31.206 --> 16:36.168
and said, is there any way I can renew this?

16:36.668 --> 16:43.390
And I said, no, surely by now we would have been able to find somebody.

16:45.331 --> 16:55.694
And we discussed, and I started the paperwork, and I still remember the lady went to talk to her boss, I guess, and came back and said,

16:56.638 --> 16:57.981
There is one possible.

16:58.181 --> 17:02.230
If you can find a teaching job, you can stay.

17:03.433 --> 17:06.680
We'll give you whatever it's called.

17:09.159 --> 17:19.387
It's not the green card, it's definitely a work permit and also a temporary status, I guess.

17:20.208 --> 17:22.589
But you can't leave New Hampshire.

17:22.609 --> 17:25.992
I said, okay, I can leave.

17:26.352 --> 17:29.674
But that was kind of mid-July.

17:30.795 --> 17:34.577
And I go, ooh, school starts in, what, four weeks?

17:36.398 --> 17:38.880
And that was marvelous.

17:38.980 --> 17:50.347
I was in a little town in Peterborough, New Hampshire, and four different families went all around looking for, you know, do you need me?

17:51.187 --> 17:55.049
And they...

17:56.990 --> 17:58.651
I had essentially given up.

17:58.811 --> 18:02.152
I started making reservations to go back.

18:03.552 --> 18:14.435
And I got one of my friends who was a retired Air Force officer colonel.

18:16.216 --> 18:19.898
called me and said, I got somebody, but you gotta hop.

18:20.238 --> 18:21.919
I said, okay.

18:22.800 --> 18:34.106
Turns out it's a junior college and their math professor, a lady, turned really sick.

18:34.806 --> 18:35.487
And she said,

18:37.299 --> 18:45.103
And I said, well, we need somebody to teach you calculus one and two, right?

18:46.263 --> 18:51.305
Okay, I haven't done that, but I did study that way back.

18:54.050 --> 19:08.822
And so he was the president of the college, not president, director, yeah, must have been president of the college, wrote a letter and said, take that to immigration.

19:09.222 --> 19:12.524
And the same lady said, well, okay, I'm impressed.

19:12.544 --> 19:13.225
So now I had

19:17.628 --> 19:23.633
And that was valid for two years, I think, yeah.

19:25.314 --> 19:35.382
And so, and as I said, I had met my wife there, which, so I had now all kinds of reasons for, it'd be nice.

19:35.823 --> 19:38.445
I asked her, it turned out she's from Germany.

19:39.626 --> 19:40.887
which is interesting.

19:42.488 --> 19:48.993
She's much younger than I am, so we were the start of the year old.

19:55.018 --> 20:08.758
Yeah, when we decided, when we got serious with each other, we went to Europe to meet our respective in-laws.

20:12.021 --> 20:19.083
My mother had lots of friends, a lot of friends.

20:19.263 --> 20:26.265
She was working as a translator for German and English to French.

20:28.259 --> 20:38.041
And I knew several of her friends were Jewish and had serious problems during the war.

20:39.342 --> 20:42.042
And here I come with a German girl.

20:42.402 --> 20:46.704
And I thought, well, wait, she's much younger than I am.

20:47.404 --> 20:48.724
That was not true.

20:49.044 --> 20:56.966
It certainly wasn't her fault, but even, you know, she was born toward, after the war was over.

20:57.546 --> 21:07.488
So I had all kinds of reasons prepared, but I had forgotten how the French, especially the Parisians, think.

21:08.529 --> 21:11.089
And they didn't say, but she's German.

21:11.429 --> 21:15.310
They said, wait, she isn't French.

21:15.630 --> 21:16.991
I wasn't prepared for that.

21:19.793 --> 21:26.202
Yeah, well, I didn't go to the U.S. to specifically look for a French girl.

21:27.203 --> 21:28.005
That was funny.

21:28.085 --> 21:32.211
But we got along with our respective in-laws.

21:35.733 --> 21:42.395
So after the two years in the junior college, I decided to go back to school.

21:44.336 --> 21:53.458
So I got a student visa, University of New Hampshire, electrical engineering, and I got bachelor's and master's from there.

21:54.318 --> 22:07.269
And in the process, somebody said, you know, you're legal with your student status, but you've been here for so long, why don't you try applying for a green card?

22:09.311 --> 22:10.912
And so I did.

22:13.235 --> 22:26.114
And it's when I was a senior at the university, I got a letter saying, okay, we've got the Green Cup, but you've got to register for the draft.

22:29.437 --> 22:32.759
Oh, the middle of the Vietnam War, right?

22:34.620 --> 22:44.465
So I went there, and I still remember the master sergeant who was interviewing me.

22:44.485 --> 22:50.949
He said, yeah, okay, so what's your background, et cetera, et cetera.

22:51.329 --> 22:54.291
He said, you know, I just did three years military.

22:54.551 --> 22:55.792
He looked at me, he said,

22:56.492 --> 22:57.753
Wrong army, buddy.

22:59.414 --> 23:01.294
Yeah.

23:01.515 --> 23:01.995
I admit.

23:02.535 --> 23:03.896
So sign up here.

23:04.756 --> 23:15.761
And then toward the end, because then I had to take a physical and this, and then I came back to him and he said, you forgot to write your birth date.

23:17.022 --> 23:17.822
Yeah, sorry.

23:18.083 --> 23:32.509
I said, wait, you're over 27, so you're right, we need you to register, but unless we declare officially a war, you're too old.

23:33.590 --> 23:39.612
Okay, I guess that's a compliment.

23:39.893 --> 23:44.975
So about, I don't know, several months later, I got the green card, and

23:47.001 --> 23:51.842
Dang, you get to serve in one army and then almost.

23:52.680 --> 23:56.482
Yeah, and I wasn't, I had to think about that.

23:56.962 --> 24:01.585
Do I really want to go to Vietnam and fight over there?

24:02.245 --> 24:04.306
Well, yeah, I think it's worth it.

24:04.406 --> 24:10.069
Besides, my wife had no interest in going back to live in Germany.

24:11.450 --> 24:12.851
So she's staying here.

24:12.871 --> 24:16.152
I think I'm staying here, if possible.

24:17.453 --> 24:18.954
What was, did your wife work?

24:19.565 --> 24:33.272
Yeah, well, when I met her, she was working in a pharmacy, not as a pharmacist, but to help the customers and things.

24:35.332 --> 24:42.175
And then when I decided to go to the university, she, for some reason, followed me.

24:44.636 --> 24:50.379
And she worked in the pharmacy there, but she was working part-time and going to secretary school.

24:55.102 --> 24:57.925
So she was also really busy.

24:57.965 --> 24:59.667
She had a green card already.

25:01.208 --> 25:06.273
She came as, you know what au pair means?

25:06.394 --> 25:07.054
Yeah.

25:08.095 --> 25:09.577
She was a major, I think.

25:14.261 --> 25:26.090
for whom she had babysitted his kids while he was stationed in Germany and done other things.

25:26.290 --> 25:28.551
And so they knew each other very well.

25:28.711 --> 25:32.754
And he said, come on over.

25:33.715 --> 25:39.219
And then she was au pair with him for, I don't know, three years, something like that.

25:39.639 --> 25:41.340
A good amount of time.

25:41.400 --> 25:41.901
Yeah, yeah.

25:44.164 --> 25:45.594
So, university...

25:47.792 --> 25:49.573
Yeah, bachelor's, master's.

25:51.334 --> 26:09.363
And then I worked for what was then General Telephone, which is now Verizon in Massachusetts, just outside of Boston.

26:11.063 --> 26:15.125
I was with them for five years.

26:17.573 --> 26:22.216
and then did something I wanted to do for decades.

26:22.337 --> 26:25.879
I wanted to go overseas and help the people.

26:25.899 --> 26:45.213
I mean, I'm not a doctor, and I'm not, but if they need help with telecom, maybe I can do training and things like that.

26:46.633 --> 26:55.917
And so I had applied to the UN as a specialized agency called the International Telecommunication Union.

26:56.818 --> 27:09.423
And one branch of that is development for less developed countries.

27:12.279 --> 27:24.612
So I applied, I had applied already coming out of the radio school and they said, well, we don't take people at the technician level.

27:26.634 --> 27:31.659
So once I got bachelor's degree, I reapplied and they said, yeah, that's good.

27:34.942 --> 27:45.391
But we prefer, since you're going in those countries, you really need another, I mean, a higher degree.

27:46.673 --> 27:48.915
And it turned out they were absolutely right.

27:50.936 --> 27:55.040
In most of these countries, people will not take...

27:57.051 --> 28:05.357
I don't want to call it orders, but directives from somebody who is not at a higher level than they are.

28:05.457 --> 28:06.938
And they were absolutely right.

28:08.078 --> 28:13.021
So I got my master's and I applied again.

28:14.142 --> 28:40.273
and it's very good we'd like to have you but since you're going to be the specialist over there they call it an expert which is unfortunate because then you go there and well you're expert in everything telecom so no i never worked at those guys they said you were there anyhow so uh you know you you have some experience in the military and some but

28:42.774 --> 28:48.936
again, you need to have a lot of experience, otherwise they're not going to listen to you.

28:50.697 --> 28:52.938
So five years, ooh.

28:53.598 --> 28:56.639
So that's where I went to work for General Telephone.

28:57.660 --> 29:03.202
So after, and while I was there, after the five years, I reapplied.

29:03.362 --> 29:04.863
No, I actually didn't.

29:05.623 --> 29:18.168
I got a letter from the ITU saying we got, we revised your, not revised, we reviewed your application and we noticed you got five years now.

29:19.128 --> 29:20.149
We'd like to have you

29:21.189 --> 29:23.490
there are three possible positions for you.

29:23.670 --> 29:25.670
And one was Equatorial Guinea.

29:28.091 --> 29:34.153
And you know, it's, as the name indicates, they're right on the equator, right in Africa.

29:35.013 --> 29:42.655
And so we think temperature and humidity here is bad, but over there, and they don't have air conditioning.

29:43.175 --> 29:48.597
People were telling me, no, we changed shirts at least twice a day, if not three times.

29:49.877 --> 29:52.478
And I didn't want to go there alone, right?

29:52.558 --> 29:57.560
So by then we had, the twins were born, so no.

29:58.100 --> 29:59.140
Okay, what's the other?

29:59.720 --> 30:05.062
Saudi Arabia with my wife and two daughters?

30:05.963 --> 30:06.623
No, sorry.

30:07.483 --> 30:09.904
Well, we have one in Jamaica.

30:10.804 --> 30:13.868
Ah, that sounds good.

30:14.849 --> 30:16.471
So I took that job.

30:17.232 --> 30:18.473
That was for two years.

30:18.533 --> 30:20.595
They asked me to stay for an extra year.

30:21.616 --> 30:28.704
Then as I was ready to come back to the U.S., my

30:30.467 --> 30:40.404
Boss's boss in Geneva, where the specialized agency is, actually came and said, we started something in Haiti.

30:41.065 --> 30:42.908
We need somebody who speaks French.

30:45.243 --> 30:47.405
Well, I think I qualify with that.

30:49.386 --> 30:51.268
But Haiti, yeah.

30:51.868 --> 31:04.437
He said, hey, how about take your wife and kids and come for, while you help us write the proposal, they can go around.

31:06.058 --> 31:11.822
Well, and that was during Baby Doc, the dictator there.

31:13.213 --> 31:18.096
But Jamaica had its own problem.

31:19.897 --> 31:31.383
The two major political parties, and they were literally fighting each other, and it was, it was, they,

31:33.233 --> 31:36.956
they left foreigners alone, which is good.

31:38.316 --> 31:50.865
But there were a whole region of Kingston, the capital of Jamaica, where, you know, just don't go there.

31:50.885 --> 31:56.669
So we get to Haiti, and it's

31:58.815 --> 32:02.983
I mean, wait, what's going on?

32:03.083 --> 32:04.706
It's so calm here.

32:04.806 --> 32:10.376
Of course, found out later why he'd get out of, you know.

32:11.914 --> 32:15.855
baby dog, full dictatorship, and so you don't mess around.

32:16.396 --> 32:27.959
But I remember walking with him one evening, and we kind of lost our way, and we were in the harbor area, and I thought, oh, that's not a good idea.

32:28.780 --> 32:37.503
And people came to ask us, not for money, or what the heck, are you doing it, but are you lost?

32:37.523 --> 32:38.383
Can we help you?

32:39.640 --> 32:41.702
That's different.

32:41.722 --> 32:44.224
So based on that, many other things.

32:44.304 --> 32:46.386
The job was very interesting.

32:48.788 --> 33:04.140
So after discussion with my wife and then three daughters, we went there for three years, which ended up being six.

33:06.722 --> 33:10.545
So I did a lot of training and organizations.

33:13.568 --> 33:20.274
And then my next post was going to be, still with the UN, it was going to be in Brazil.

33:22.095 --> 33:28.021
And I told him by then, he was my direct boss, now he was in Geneva.

33:28.781 --> 33:30.342
because I was project manager.

33:31.523 --> 33:33.444
And I said, I don't speak Portuguese.

33:33.544 --> 33:36.246
I said, you're going to a research center.

33:36.526 --> 33:38.007
They all speak English.

33:38.627 --> 33:38.887
Good.

33:39.728 --> 33:41.409
But wait a minute.

33:42.970 --> 33:47.753
Can I have some details of, you know, research center?

33:47.773 --> 33:49.554
Yeah, telecom research center.

33:49.594 --> 33:50.755
They're pretty advanced.

33:51.575 --> 33:52.916
They have PhDs.

33:55.678 --> 33:56.999
What do you want me for?

33:57.019 --> 34:00.802
I don't have a PhD.

34:02.524 --> 34:06.687
We've got all kinds of people, researchers.

34:07.027 --> 34:13.352
What we need is something to keep them together, and we need a team leader.

34:13.772 --> 34:16.855
You've been the project manager there for three years.

34:19.330 --> 34:20.871
Okay, if you say so.

34:21.631 --> 34:25.133
So I talked to my wife and the kids.

34:25.373 --> 34:28.375
They said, no.

34:28.495 --> 34:31.676
By that time, the twins were 17.

34:32.877 --> 34:37.319
We are going back to the U.S. You can go where you want.

34:37.539 --> 34:40.020
Okay, get overruled.

34:41.301 --> 34:42.842
So we came back to the U.S.

34:44.946 --> 34:49.750
And well, it's time to look for a job.

34:49.870 --> 34:51.671
And one of our

34:55.009 --> 35:03.754
Specialized Magazine, the IEEE, the Institute of Electronic and Electrical Engineering.

35:03.814 --> 35:06.495
They have many magazines.

35:07.456 --> 35:21.103
And I looked at the job openings, the opening in Texas here, and I said, hmm, for telecoms specifically.

35:22.164 --> 35:23.044
That's unusual.

35:26.047 --> 35:26.788
That sounds good.

35:29.169 --> 35:34.212
So we came and visited.

35:34.232 --> 35:42.338
I got interviewed and I liked it.

35:43.338 --> 35:49.442
So I had to convince all my girls.

35:52.384 --> 35:59.567
And one argument I use is to say, look, this is a nine-month contract, so I've got three months of vacation.

35:59.687 --> 36:01.688
Think of all the time we're going to spend together.

36:02.648 --> 36:04.889
Forty years later, they still remind me.

36:04.949 --> 36:07.650
So when I was spending all this time together...

36:08.653 --> 36:14.255
I say, yeah, it's a nine-month contract, but you do have 12 months of work.

36:14.375 --> 36:19.698
But I mean, I don't regret it at all.

36:21.358 --> 36:27.721
Admittedly, if I had known ahead of time, I probably wouldn't have taken it.

36:29.862 --> 36:31.663
But I'm glad I did.

36:31.803 --> 36:33.103
Yeah, absolutely.

36:34.084 --> 36:36.425
It's, I mean, that...

36:38.076 --> 36:43.322
So, 19 years, 19 and a half years here teaching.

36:44.564 --> 36:44.804
Wow.

36:45.344 --> 36:46.946
We've been everywhere it seems.

36:47.967 --> 36:55.936
The summers were, you know, taken, well, one thing, as I say, telecom, technology was...

36:57.457 --> 37:20.935
increasing and improving very quickly so one part of my summer is to catch up with this because I should teach them and that takes a long time then the books are by definition at least a year behind usually two because it takes a year to write it and then

37:24.597 --> 37:37.082
So I put my own notes based on what I had learned in conferences and in specialized magazines and get copyrights and things like that.

37:38.363 --> 37:39.724
That takes a long time.

37:40.384 --> 37:45.946
And then you have to find a way to get scholarship for these people.

37:46.646 --> 37:49.288
You can't do that during the year because you...

37:50.748 --> 37:53.970
and then also equipment for the lab.

37:54.450 --> 37:59.592
So the summers were pretty busy.

38:00.913 --> 38:11.257
Ellie, my wife, would often say, no, wait a minute, industry, you had at least two weeks guaranteed, right?

38:11.357 --> 38:15.079
Three, but here you can't?

38:15.319 --> 38:16.739
No, I've got to do that.

38:21.562 --> 38:32.770
I figured with all the summer months where I was either not paid at all or half salary,

38:35.168 --> 38:38.629
I essentially worked nine and a half months for free.

38:38.989 --> 38:42.530
Not consecutively, but over the...

38:43.371 --> 38:47.052
Which, again, I don't regret.

38:47.092 --> 38:54.194
When I left, we had three labs, telecom labs.

38:55.174 --> 38:58.595
with equipment, a couple million dollars worth of equipment.

39:00.235 --> 39:07.137
When I came, there was half a lab, and then two courses.

39:07.157 --> 39:13.098
I left, there were nine courses, because each year I would add an additional lab.

39:13.958 --> 39:15.898
That also takes time, by the way.

39:17.679 --> 39:22.300
But I had several offers from industry as I went along,

39:23.880 --> 39:45.695
So, obviously I stayed, and I guess I thoroughly enjoy the relationship with the students, and I felt, you know, former students, some of them still email me, or we meet somewhere, and they're grateful, and we thank you so much.

39:46.896 --> 39:48.597
I thought you hated my test.

39:49.157 --> 39:51.699
I did, but they were very useful.

39:51.999 --> 39:52.520
Yeah.

39:53.692 --> 40:00.395
There's something to be said about that.

40:00.495 --> 40:03.016
I really don't regret it at all.

40:04.156 --> 40:05.837
So that's good.

40:07.457 --> 40:14.800
So the process of, because you said you came and there wasn't like a full program, so you helped build the program.

40:15.120 --> 40:15.822
Yeah, yeah.

40:16.162 --> 40:17.084
That was my role.

40:17.204 --> 40:22.212
That was my, I guess, it was official title in the department.

40:22.232 --> 40:28.062
I don't know if it was true in the college, but I was telecommunication course leader.

40:29.433 --> 40:35.294
because I was developing all those, which is fine.

40:36.875 --> 40:45.457
And I started as an assistant prof, but of course then you got to do a lot of research and publishing.

40:46.877 --> 40:50.558
And I was reminded of that by the...

40:55.260 --> 40:57.081
by the department head, right?

40:58.723 --> 41:00.524
Several of them over the years.

41:03.086 --> 41:09.332
And they said, we love you here, and you're doing a good job, but you've got to do research.

41:09.352 --> 41:12.394
I said, that's not what I came for.

41:12.514 --> 41:13.615
I came for teaching.

41:13.976 --> 41:17.579
Research, I can get a lot better funding in industry.

41:18.657 --> 41:22.500
And they said, yeah, but I can't keep you as assistant prof.

41:23.601 --> 41:28.245
I said, well, any alternative?

41:28.565 --> 41:32.709
And I reminded them, you know, this is an accredited program, right?

41:32.849 --> 41:35.111
And I think I had something to do.

41:35.171 --> 41:38.954
He said, absolutely, but the rule is, which is true.

41:40.075 --> 41:41.957
And he said, well, you can be a lecturer.

41:44.059 --> 41:45.920
I was a lecturer in grad school.

41:47.608 --> 41:49.269
How about senior lecturer?

41:51.431 --> 41:52.012
Let me check.

41:52.352 --> 41:56.275
Then he came back from the dean and said, yeah, okay, you're on.

41:57.616 --> 41:57.997
All right.

41:58.477 --> 42:00.919
But you realize you can never get tenure.

42:03.101 --> 42:05.002
I didn't come for that.

42:05.022 --> 42:09.706
If I'm in the ministry, they can kick me off in two weeks, right?

42:10.267 --> 42:14.150
Here, I've got that list at the end of the semester, so always.

42:18.344 --> 42:24.090
I didn't realize that you would still have to do research.

42:24.411 --> 42:25.291
Oh, yeah.

42:25.392 --> 42:29.396
If you want tenure, there's no research and publishing.

42:29.416 --> 42:32.980
You've got to publish books, or at least articles, and preferably books.

42:34.115 --> 42:50.150
And I had started writing a book on testing telecommunication equipment because, well, I've used those, I've seen those, but...

42:51.881 --> 42:54.062
So the students need it when they get there.

42:55.382 --> 42:58.063
But that takes a lot of time.

42:58.443 --> 43:06.265
And after the second chapter, I had already contacted the editor who was interested.

43:07.086 --> 43:15.708
I said, you know, I know I told you I would do that, but it's either I continue with that at the expense of the students.

43:15.728 --> 43:16.649
You can't do that.

43:17.870 --> 43:24.184
So, that was another reason why it stayed.

43:26.048 --> 43:27.731
Okay, from a perspective,

43:29.690 --> 43:36.913
from a, what's the right word?

43:37.273 --> 43:46.476
Sure, if you have an assistant associate prof, that sounds much better than senior lecturer, but who cares?

43:47.157 --> 43:50.218
This is not what I came here for.

43:51.261 --> 43:54.965
So you really enjoy the teaching aspect of it.

43:55.105 --> 43:56.006
Oh yeah, totally.

43:57.387 --> 43:59.609
Yes, very much so.

43:59.830 --> 44:05.555
And again, you know, I had a close, of course, the telecom...

44:07.694 --> 44:21.262
Typically, I had in each course something like between 20 and 25 students, so you can know them, you can relate both ways.

44:22.823 --> 44:30.188
In fact, I told the seniors the first time, I said, okay, now that you're seniors, you can call me Pierre.

44:31.209 --> 44:32.409
And they loved that.

44:33.250 --> 44:33.930
One of my...

44:37.453 --> 44:42.098
Yeah, I think his title was coordinator.

44:42.178 --> 44:45.582
It's an assistant department head type thing.

44:46.602 --> 45:01.049
who somehow one of the students mentioned that, you know, they call it, and he was a retired Air Force guy, recently retired, and he said, that's not acceptable.

45:02.870 --> 45:07.873
And he told the students, don't ever do that again, because students came and said, oh, yeah.

45:08.733 --> 45:10.794
I said, let me go talk.

45:11.354 --> 45:13.236
And he gave me a hard time.

45:13.256 --> 45:16.857
I said, you're going to lose respect.

45:18.358 --> 45:25.162
The only reason they respect me is if I'm called by my last name, then I, you know.

45:26.343 --> 45:30.024
I said, no, but it's just not done, which is true.

45:31.725 --> 45:34.767
And then the department had overheard the discussion.

45:34.787 --> 45:38.469
He said to him, he said, you're not in

45:43.596 --> 45:46.399
So that continued, and the seniors loved it.

45:46.860 --> 45:51.705
That was their graduation to senior level.

45:53.087 --> 45:53.968
And I, yeah, I,

45:57.843 --> 46:17.535
When I started, I started with, there was an introductory course to telecom for the sophomores, and then a poorly defined telephone request.

46:19.748 --> 46:20.909
network, let's say.

46:23.090 --> 46:28.032
But as I said, that expanded a lot.

46:28.292 --> 46:40.838
I had an industrial advisory board like most programs do, but we were president or equivalent of telephone companies all over Texas and others.

46:41.238 --> 46:45.400
You know, Southwestern Bell, NGT, and...

46:47.861 --> 47:13.735
When I came at first, the first semester, it's a long, I don't know if I'm diverging too much, but these people involved with telephone companies had asked, I guess, the then College of Engineering

47:20.763 --> 47:39.387
I'm trying to remember, well, it won't be an exact thing, but they said, look, we need some telecom people, you know, sure, you're sending us electronic, you know, electrical, electronic people, but then we have to train them for six months, because that's, you don't teach this stuff.

47:40.348 --> 47:45.629
And the then dean of the College of Engineering, this is 1985, right?

47:47.429 --> 47:49.870
Well, no, in fact, it was before I came, so now...

47:51.751 --> 47:57.536
and said, oh, but what you want us to do, that's not university level.

47:57.616 --> 47:59.858
That's a technician program.

48:00.098 --> 48:04.541
No, no, we want engineers, but we want a specialized engineer.

48:04.962 --> 48:06.263
Sorry, we can't do that.

48:06.343 --> 48:07.324
We don't do that here.

48:09.165 --> 48:27.141
And so, I don't remember the details since I wasn't there, I'm just trying to recollect, but somebody in the college said to them, said, wait, why don't you try the engineering technology department?

48:27.261 --> 48:31.444
They are not training people for grad school, they're training people for industry.

48:32.425 --> 48:40.590
So they went, and so in engineering and technology, they said, yeah, well, we can do that.

48:42.631 --> 48:46.113
So they started that one and a half course I'm talking about.

48:47.634 --> 48:57.861
And so when I came, those same people, presidents of different sales companies came, and we had a meeting, and that's,

48:58.821 --> 49:04.206
I introduced myself and said, there are several ways I can orient that.

49:04.746 --> 49:07.348
I did graduate from electronic programs.

49:07.929 --> 49:17.456
Are you interested in them designing circuits or are you interested in them applying the existing equipment in the best possible?

49:18.037 --> 49:20.999
I said, circuits we have from electrical engineers.

49:21.059 --> 49:22.200
We got plenty of those.

49:22.280 --> 49:23.561
That's not what we want.

49:23.641 --> 49:26.484
We don't want to have spent six months training everybody.

49:27.304 --> 49:32.166
Oh, good, that's where I came from, system side.

49:32.346 --> 49:43.570
And they were, so that I developed the different courses based on my experience, but also saying, okay, but what do you people want more specifically?

49:43.610 --> 49:46.191
What kind of technology are you using?

49:46.271 --> 49:51.712
And so we started things to respond to that.

49:53.773 --> 50:02.783
Because they were pleased with the department and with the graduates, they started offering some equipment.

50:04.125 --> 50:08.329
And I remember that was after several years.

50:08.389 --> 50:15.117
But I kept a relationship with those guys, and we were fairly close.

50:16.810 --> 50:28.358
And I asked one of them, I said, I won't mention the company, but I said, you know, I'm impressed by the amount of equipment you're giving us and the scholarships.

50:28.518 --> 50:32.241
He said, well, there's something special about your program.

50:33.682 --> 50:40.087
We noticed your equipment within a semester or so has been set up and is working.

50:41.127 --> 50:43.129
We know you're doing something with it.

50:43.369 --> 50:48.474
There are other places where that thing just sits there for whatever reason, for years.

50:48.494 --> 50:51.516
I said, well, I can keep doing that.

50:51.576 --> 50:53.158
Keep doing that.

50:54.059 --> 50:58.302
So, and yeah, I mean, and the scholarships, too.

50:58.622 --> 50:59.063
That was...

51:00.404 --> 51:11.466
And because, well, okay, I get some credit there, because the program was good and correctly oriented.

51:13.628 --> 51:25.418
And because telecom was booming at that time, all my students, if they had C or better, had a job before they graduated.

51:25.498 --> 51:36.988
Some of them went on the internship, but even besides that, they came here and they would tell, which didn't please the recruiting folks over there.

51:37.468 --> 51:42.615
They said, no, no, no, we want to talk to the engineering technology telecom group.

51:43.676 --> 51:44.598
But we have many.

51:44.678 --> 51:45.339
Yes, we know.

51:45.359 --> 51:48.202
Everybody got a job.

51:52.928 --> 51:54.189
You had to have a seat.

51:54.230 --> 51:55.831
That's fair enough, right?

51:55.871 --> 51:59.134
That's impressive.

52:00.575 --> 52:09.882
One of the petroleum company, they had their own, like all of a large corporation, they have their own telecom network, privately owned.

52:11.224 --> 52:14.246
So they have a telecom group that takes care of that.

52:15.427 --> 52:20.992
And their director of that group...

52:23.033 --> 52:29.637
Now you ask them, I would go and visit them, that particular thing was in Chicago.

52:31.023 --> 52:38.647
And I asked him over dinner, he said, you know, I'm really grateful that you are hiring my C students.

52:39.828 --> 52:52.454
And they said, well, but I've told our recruiting folks, if they come from your program, add a letter to that, go up.

52:52.535 --> 52:55.776
So your Cs are my Bs, and that's fine.

52:55.836 --> 52:57.797
That's high praise.

52:57.817 --> 52:59.078
I like that, yeah.

53:03.302 --> 53:06.703
Yeah, it was great.

53:08.043 --> 53:16.165
Well, you know, there's a big satellite antenna next to Tom, so that was given to us.

53:18.425 --> 53:22.226
And we used that until I left, I guess.

53:25.286 --> 53:30.388
I hesitate to open the door and see what's inside, because I know it hasn't been used since.

53:33.348 --> 53:36.690
But, yeah, I mean, it was great.

53:37.170 --> 53:37.770
It was great.

53:38.611 --> 53:44.473
How did you keep up with, since the technology was changing so quickly, how did you keep up with that?

53:44.493 --> 53:47.635
Yeah, well, that was strenuous, I would say.

53:49.075 --> 53:50.576
One thing is conferences.

53:50.696 --> 53:54.218
I would go in the summer and sometimes during the year as well.

53:55.919 --> 53:58.740
Big telecoms, some of them at the national level,

53:59.777 --> 54:07.789
conferences and people present their new stuff and you meet people that can help you.

54:09.932 --> 54:12.797
If you have any questions, just give me a call, do it.

54:14.779 --> 54:17.920
A lot of that I didn't know.

54:18.220 --> 54:19.521
You have to learn.

54:21.582 --> 54:24.303
So that was one big thing.

54:25.783 --> 54:35.567
The other thing is I had the possibility that I could go there and just

54:36.607 --> 54:54.774
spend the day in the company looking around talking to their engineers and things like that that helps a lot and they would say you know there there is this thing again that was not internet yet we had just email but

54:59.574 --> 55:05.801
And specialized magazines.

55:07.142 --> 55:09.945
But yeah, it takes a lot of time.

55:10.025 --> 55:14.730
And some of the staff say, wait a minute, I better go ask somebody here.

55:15.911 --> 55:16.752
How does that work?

55:20.723 --> 55:34.318
But I was thoroughly interested in that, so that you're willing to go deeper and longer than you would otherwise.

55:35.178 --> 55:47.549
I remember talking with one of my colleagues from the English department, and I said, you guys are lucky.

55:50.291 --> 55:51.992
Things don't change, right?

55:52.953 --> 55:57.237
And so you've got three months off in the summer.

55:57.697 --> 55:58.878
He said, yeah.

56:01.172 --> 56:09.337
You know how boring it is to teach Shakespeare's to freshmen again and again, and they couldn't care less.

56:10.537 --> 56:19.022
Which is a good point, because by, since, except the first four years, all the courses I was teaching were at the junior and senior level.

56:20.477 --> 56:24.320
And if they've reached that point, obviously they're interested.

56:24.620 --> 56:29.064
And that makes it, we had some very interesting discussion.

56:29.084 --> 56:33.687
I used, not every year, but for quite a while.

56:34.548 --> 56:37.050
I had, once a week, I had an evening thing.

56:37.110 --> 56:38.711
You guys come and ask questions.

56:38.791 --> 56:43.875
We just, you know, and we'd bring pizza and just discuss whatever they're interested in.

56:45.416 --> 56:46.337
And they...

56:48.777 --> 56:50.458
So I was never bored.

56:51.959 --> 56:52.660
Never bored.

56:53.340 --> 57:01.506
You seem like the type of professor that students would seek out, that they would actually go to, feel comfortable with.

57:02.200 --> 57:05.502
Well, and open office doors.

57:05.602 --> 57:07.624
If I'm there, the door is open.

57:07.724 --> 57:11.947
Unless there's a student that comes in and has some problem.

57:12.547 --> 57:16.570
He or she doesn't want other people to hear.

57:16.690 --> 57:19.832
But that helped, too.

57:22.154 --> 57:26.477
There were many national-level conferences, but one in particular...

57:28.038 --> 57:38.186
was specifically for the telecom, I think, for the large companies that had their own private telecom.

57:38.226 --> 57:44.411
So there's a whole group of people that you don't, well, the general public

57:44.931 --> 57:45.772
doesn't think of.

57:45.852 --> 57:49.576
That's a telephone company or the internet company.

57:50.237 --> 58:05.493
But they have very elaborate networks and they usually move with the technology faster than the telephone companies do because of budget and other reasons.

58:06.394 --> 58:06.574
So

58:09.201 --> 58:18.346
So I almost always went to these groups.

58:19.606 --> 58:27.170
And after a while, in fact, when we became accredited, I got invited and said, you can bring four of your students.

58:29.751 --> 58:32.153
And so I would pick my four top students.

58:32.233 --> 58:34.114
And I told them ahead of time, you know,

58:38.191 --> 58:57.101
I'm not going to, this is not a condition, but if I take just my four students and they can come with me on this, so they have to pay for the airplane thing, but the conference is free, the room is free, the food is free.

58:58.322 --> 58:59.523
And you're going to be...

59:00.963 --> 59:05.306
in an environment where you have the most recent equipment.

59:07.568 --> 59:08.469
That helped too.

59:08.569 --> 59:16.474
And of course, I would meet these other telecom managers and, oh, we didn't know you had the telecom program.

59:16.494 --> 59:17.295
Yeah, it was good.

59:21.832 --> 59:23.593
That's good.

59:26.154 --> 59:37.120
When the department offered me a nice retirement reception in the former student,

59:42.563 --> 59:56.578
And so, you know, the speech and thing, and in mind, I used the occasion to publicly apologize to my daughters and wife for never being there.

59:57.499 --> 59:59.261
Never, but that's true.

01:00:00.811 --> 01:00:15.479
Yeah, that was, I retired two more times, and then they said, well, we're not offering you, no, that was so good, that's so well done, that was worth three.

01:00:17.661 --> 01:00:24.264
This seems silly that I haven't asked this yet, but how did you first get interested in telecom?

01:00:24.324 --> 01:00:28.807
Ham radio, yeah, that was definitely...

01:00:31.232 --> 01:00:33.215
Yes, that was the start.

01:00:33.755 --> 01:00:45.331
I started going to meetings, ham radio meetings, and then met others, and then got my...

01:00:47.012 --> 01:00:49.853
In France, you had to build your own transmitter too.

01:00:51.974 --> 01:01:09.400
But, so, that was, and I had a mentor, if you want to have, and then, that's why I went to that radio school after high school, rather than wherever.

01:01:09.820 --> 01:01:11.480
Interesting.

01:01:15.314 --> 01:01:21.259
Something, I'm not saying it's simple, but something as simple as a hand radio got you

01:01:22.935 --> 01:01:26.877
all the way from your country, all the way over here, and all these opportunities.

01:01:26.938 --> 01:01:27.658
It's so interesting.

01:01:27.838 --> 01:01:44.008
Well, and that first work permit, Wesleyan Radio Guy, and it changed my life, really, for something very good.

01:01:49.227 --> 01:01:54.171
And you come to a brand new place, right?

01:01:54.431 --> 01:01:55.832
And you know nobody.

01:01:58.275 --> 01:02:02.578
Just pick up your radio and talk through the repeaters.

01:02:02.598 --> 01:02:04.800
Oh, you know, come to our meetings.

01:02:04.900 --> 01:02:10.625
And so suddenly you've got four or five, six people that are there talking.

01:02:12.223 --> 01:02:17.306
common interest and they know this and that.

01:02:18.066 --> 01:02:20.908
So, yeah, that helped me considerably.

01:02:22.809 --> 01:02:23.990
That's awesome.

01:02:24.010 --> 01:02:25.530
That's interesting.

01:02:25.651 --> 01:02:31.874
And it's even in the military service that I was, well,

01:02:33.995 --> 01:02:43.863
I first went to NCO school and became a sergeant, but that was in the Signal Corps, so in radio.

01:02:44.823 --> 01:02:52.749
And then I was based not far from Paris because I graduated

01:02:54.231 --> 01:02:59.214
I was the second or third in line when I graduated from NCO school.

01:02:59.734 --> 01:03:03.456
So you had choices, right?

01:03:03.497 --> 01:03:08.160
The first guy, okay, what's your preference for the first year?

01:03:08.260 --> 01:03:10.701
After that, we'll send you when we need you.

01:03:11.422 --> 01:03:15.905
And I was like third, something like that.

01:03:17.405 --> 01:03:19.646
definitely among the first ten.

01:03:21.246 --> 01:03:36.311
And the guy said, well, sorry, all the places in Germany, because at that time, you know, there was the British, the Americans, the French, the, but no more space there.

01:03:36.811 --> 01:03:43.673
And there's also, oh, there's a place outside of Paris that

01:03:45.533 --> 01:03:47.434
That's our telecom training center.

01:03:48.895 --> 01:03:49.735
That sounds good.

01:03:52.037 --> 01:03:56.799
And I didn't tell him that, especially my parents live just across.

01:03:58.240 --> 01:04:01.662
So you're half an hour on bicycle.

01:04:02.803 --> 01:04:05.124
Oh yeah.

01:04:05.324 --> 01:04:06.284
I was lucky there.

01:04:06.685 --> 01:04:08.185
And in fact,

01:04:13.748 --> 01:04:21.470
In that time, military service was mandatory in France, so you had to go.

01:04:22.230 --> 01:04:31.252
But they would take you wherever they needed so that you could find yourself, regardless of your background, you know, as a truck driver or whatever.

01:04:32.153 --> 01:04:38.254
But one of the hams in a monthly meeting was a...

01:04:41.195 --> 01:04:51.726
At that time, he was either major or lieutenant colonel, but he was, we saw him often.

01:04:51.746 --> 01:04:56.030
He came, I guess he was stationed not far from Paris either.

01:04:57.151 --> 01:05:03.498
And he told me and a friend who was also in the radio school, said, you know,

01:05:04.118 --> 01:05:16.911
If you want to be in the single corps, the only way that you can be sure of that is to enlist rather than wait to be constricted.

01:05:19.754 --> 01:05:24.699
So mandatory with 18 months, a year and a half.

01:05:26.531 --> 01:05:30.776
If you're enlisted, it's minimum three years.

01:05:31.336 --> 01:05:43.429
So he said, your choice, but I would recommend you do it that way, and you also, through your first year,

01:05:44.611 --> 01:05:47.914
You become a sergeant, which has some advantages.

01:05:47.934 --> 01:05:54.401
And so I talked it over with my friend and said, yep, let's go that way.

01:05:54.501 --> 01:06:00.587
And I remember my dad saying, a doctor, that's it.

01:06:02.708 --> 01:06:16.864
you're gonna win this uh yeah but oh okay so and and this so that was a him too and in fact i

01:06:18.765 --> 01:06:31.176
The third year, the last year of the service, I was in that train center doing training for people to maintain and repair high-power transmitters and things like that.

01:06:32.878 --> 01:06:37.602
And we have a new commanding officer coming in.

01:06:38.983 --> 01:06:44.688
And so, you know, we will wait for him in decent speech.

01:06:46.077 --> 01:06:48.958
I look at him, he's in full uniform, of course.

01:06:49.058 --> 01:06:52.179
I thought, I know this guy.

01:06:52.199 --> 01:06:53.679
I couldn't figure out why.

01:06:55.080 --> 01:06:56.900
And that was the guy.

01:06:56.920 --> 01:07:01.682
And so he called me in his office later and said,

01:07:05.983 --> 01:07:27.474
We know each other, and we're both hams, but you do remember that I'm, so, and it's not a personal thing, but you know, other officers are there, so please remember that I'm Colonel, so I'm, yes sir.

01:07:30.272 --> 01:07:39.497
But as a reward, there is a ham radio station on the floor above my office, which has been inactive.

01:07:40.318 --> 01:07:52.345
So you need to get that thing back on the air and use it to call and let people know that this is... That sounds like a good deal, sir.

01:07:53.245 --> 01:07:57.548
Yeah, I mean, all my life, ham radio is played...

01:07:58.622 --> 01:08:04.707
Um, the decision to enlist, that must have been scary, especially considering the time period.

01:08:06.850 --> 01:08:08.670
Yeah.

01:08:09.231 --> 01:08:22.713
Yeah, I mean, there's a, especially now, I mean, I'm 88, so, but, it's still in New Hampshire.

01:08:23.893 --> 01:08:31.755
I remember a reunion on D-Day, June 4th, sorry, June 6th, 1944.

01:08:33.595 --> 01:08:38.821
And there were several retired officers there, including the one that got me the teaching job.

01:08:39.882 --> 01:08:42.785
And I said, well, where were you on your barbecue and things?

01:08:42.805 --> 01:08:44.767
I said, where were you on D-Day?

01:08:45.167 --> 01:08:49.232
I'm in the U.S. somewhere, in the Pacific, and I said,

01:08:51.863 --> 01:09:00.232
And it comes to me, and I'm like, you know, at least 10 years younger, if not 15, than they are.

01:09:00.252 --> 01:09:03.875
I said, well, I guess I was the only one in Normandy.

01:09:04.676 --> 01:09:09.161
And the lawyer said, you enlisted age eight?

01:09:09.281 --> 01:09:10.162
You didn't ask.

01:09:10.482 --> 01:09:12.985
I wasn't on the beach, but you asked.

01:09:13.785 --> 01:09:14.846
where were you in the field?

01:09:15.066 --> 01:09:16.167
I was in Normandy.

01:09:16.187 --> 01:09:20.950
I happened to be a seven-year-old kid when you guys came along and I was glad to see you.

01:09:22.530 --> 01:09:24.151
But that was...

01:09:25.769 --> 01:09:34.254
Yeah, I mean, during the war, there were some very close calls.

01:09:34.654 --> 01:09:52.865
In fact, we moved from Paris to the south of France because, I don't know if you know that, but the armistice with the Germans said, okay, we're occupying the northern part of France and the rest is

01:09:55.174 --> 01:10:03.901
I mean, they're both France, but that's under French authority, and this is, of course, that didn't last very long.

01:10:04.261 --> 01:10:08.665
So we moved from Paris to the south of France.

01:10:10.585 --> 01:10:19.548
And then when the Nazis occupied the south part, we moved out and went.

01:10:20.349 --> 01:10:28.271
And I kidded my dad for a long time that we went to Normandy, not on the beach, right, on the small village.

01:10:29.412 --> 01:10:35.654
And I kidded him afterwards and said, of all the places, and he said,

01:10:37.399 --> 01:10:48.828
Everybody knew that the Allied weren't silly enough to try to scale the Atlantic Wall with all the protection and all the forts and all that.

01:10:48.908 --> 01:10:51.329
They're going to come from England.

01:10:52.090 --> 01:10:58.134
Well, they came from England, but directly or from the Mediterranean.

01:10:59.656 --> 01:11:00.756
And he's right.

01:11:01.097 --> 01:11:04.679
At that time, including Hitler, unfortunately, he thought...

01:11:06.540 --> 01:11:15.648
So it was quiet until about a year before 1944, because then it started.

01:11:16.288 --> 01:11:17.710
We were in a small village.

01:11:19.291 --> 01:11:23.875
There was a train line going through the village, and of course...

01:11:25.261 --> 01:11:30.523
trained with ammunitions and, you know, you name it, going back and forth.

01:11:33.704 --> 01:11:36.565
So that was dangerous by itself.

01:11:37.445 --> 01:11:49.389
But the British Air Force and the American would bomb these things, you know, because we don't want that to get... Yeah, but...

01:11:50.890 --> 01:11:52.990
So there were two...

01:11:54.951 --> 01:11:57.013
two big hills in that village.

01:11:57.713 --> 01:12:05.319
And they had caves to make Galvados the equivalent of Cognac, if you want.

01:12:06.060 --> 01:12:07.661
So pretty deep.

01:12:08.122 --> 01:12:21.212
So whenever there was an alert or you heard the bombers coming to half of the village go there, and sometimes they, certainly overnight and sometimes days and days.

01:12:22.708 --> 01:12:25.231
And some of the bumps came pretty close.

01:12:25.331 --> 01:12:27.033
I mean, we lucked out.

01:12:29.236 --> 01:12:30.097
It's, yeah.

01:12:36.312 --> 01:12:55.448
And then, you know, the German occupation of the village, and so, you know, they get all the food and, I mean, all the meat and all the good stuff, and we eat turnips and rutabagas for weeks.

01:12:57.389 --> 01:13:01.312
And you never knew what would happen.

01:13:02.893 --> 01:13:03.794
And, you know,

01:13:05.755 --> 01:13:13.237
Yeah, I mean, those two years were painful, to say the least.

01:13:13.317 --> 01:13:14.258
And we lucked out.

01:13:14.398 --> 01:13:16.858
It could have been much worse.

01:13:18.979 --> 01:13:24.101
I'm glad it turned out for the better.

01:13:24.221 --> 01:13:24.581
Yeah.

01:13:24.601 --> 01:13:29.442
And the village, fortunately, didn't have any strategic value.

01:13:34.149 --> 01:13:43.056
Our village didn't get liberated by the Americans until August something, early August.

01:13:43.096 --> 01:13:50.241
So, you know, the D-Day was June 6, right?

01:13:51.742 --> 01:13:54.604
Two months, two and a half months before they got.

01:13:55.185 --> 01:13:58.767
And meanwhile, they were fighting everywhere, right?

01:13:58.888 --> 01:14:00.649
And the Germans were still there.

01:14:00.669 --> 01:14:01.950
So...

01:14:03.930 --> 01:14:08.334
Sorry, I didn't mean to bring up anything painful.

01:14:09.695 --> 01:14:12.677
Well, yeah.

01:14:15.219 --> 01:14:30.332
It took me years before I could eat turnips and whatever, I guess, and even carrots, because you eat those every day, every day, every day.

01:14:32.512 --> 01:14:45.579
because the German officers said, you know, the butter, the meat, and what they didn't go to, you know, the NCOs, but not to us.

01:14:48.400 --> 01:14:55.944
Plus, you never know, I mean, there were all the concentration camp business, and you never know who's gonna get arrested next.

01:15:01.767 --> 01:15:17.097
In a way, I was lucky because I was only five, six, seven years old, and I didn't realize at that time how, you know, and that's one of my regrets.

01:15:17.217 --> 01:15:17.617
I never

01:15:19.922 --> 01:15:37.752
realized until much, much later how much my dad, you know, having accepted my mother and me through all the stuff that he had to get through.

01:15:37.852 --> 01:15:42.455
We owe him a lot.

01:15:42.595 --> 01:15:46.377
I think it's

01:15:47.687 --> 01:15:53.234
very brave of you to, you've gone through all of that, and then just to enlist.

01:15:53.274 --> 01:16:03.286
I think it's very, sorry, that's not a personal comment, but I think somebody who's gone through something like that and then still faces it head on, that's very brave.

01:16:04.306 --> 01:16:10.371
Now, to get back to the food problem, no, I'm sorry.

01:16:11.272 --> 01:16:17.597
I was so thin that after the liberation, the Red Cross

01:16:24.022 --> 01:16:36.949
sent me to Switzerland to go eat, really, for, I don't remember, several, maybe a couple of months.

01:16:38.110 --> 01:16:46.855
I was a farmer that accepted, you know, not really refugees, but

01:16:48.615 --> 01:16:57.838
I presume the Red Cross paid them something to do that.

01:16:59.258 --> 01:17:08.401
And yet, look, I'm 88, so I guess physical constitution was pretty good after all.

01:17:15.613 --> 01:17:22.250
What were some differences coming over to America, like even culturally,

01:17:25.169 --> 01:17:27.590
Yeah, that was a big change.

01:17:28.971 --> 01:17:33.273
But there's several things that just totally amazed me.

01:17:33.333 --> 01:17:36.955
And I mentioned four, five, six families.

01:17:37.856 --> 01:17:41.438
They knew me because I worked there, but that was it.

01:17:42.298 --> 01:17:47.922
The Air Force colonel and his family, they kind of adopted me.

01:17:48.862 --> 01:17:55.585
the others, and yet they all spent a lot of time trying to find me a teaching job.

01:17:57.126 --> 01:17:58.527
Wow.

01:17:58.727 --> 01:18:00.048
That really amazed me.

01:18:00.288 --> 01:18:00.528
The

01:18:06.117 --> 01:18:15.025
In general, the reception from, again, Peterborough is a small town, and maybe that helps.

01:18:18.328 --> 01:18:22.331
But I was, those people are really nice.

01:18:22.671 --> 01:18:29.618
And, you know, inviting me for barbecues, for this, for family occasions.

01:18:35.900 --> 01:18:48.324
Of course, I wish I just remembered their names, but I always don't.

01:18:57.632 --> 01:19:05.139
And the guy that was in another, that offered me the second job, that was in another town.

01:19:07.181 --> 01:19:13.486
But I don't think that would have happened in France.

01:19:13.926 --> 01:19:19.051
The ham radio group, yes, but outside of that, these were not hams.

01:19:19.171 --> 01:19:20.372
My boss was, but...

01:19:22.415 --> 01:19:35.534
And another thing that I wasn't paid very much, understandably, but enough to buy a second-hand car.

01:19:35.654 --> 01:19:36.736
It was old, but it was

01:19:36.876 --> 01:19:37.356
working.

01:19:38.777 --> 01:19:40.297
Whoa, that's great.

01:19:41.197 --> 01:19:49.279
And the other thing, people don't believe me when I tell them, but the gasoline was 25 cents a gallon in 1964.

01:19:49.339 --> 01:19:57.282
So, of course, we were, you know.

01:19:57.482 --> 01:20:00.482
So I was able to go around, yeah, my

01:20:03.137 --> 01:20:08.579
My work permit didn't allow me to leave New Hampshire, but New Hampshire's pretty good, pretty large.

01:20:11.740 --> 01:20:15.641
And the people were very friendly.

01:20:17.262 --> 01:20:32.387
Another thing, later on, when I started working for General Telephone, we bought a house, not right away, but, and

01:20:34.295 --> 01:20:36.836
So I was an engineer, master's degree.

01:20:38.156 --> 01:20:41.057
And the guy next to me was a truck driver.

01:20:41.277 --> 01:20:43.157
And I thought, that's amazing.

01:20:43.357 --> 01:20:45.858
It could never happen in France.

01:20:47.038 --> 01:20:50.939
A, because, I mean, it's a social structure.

01:20:51.399 --> 01:20:53.719
You can, you're just a truck driver.

01:20:54.440 --> 01:20:56.280
And the guy was very nice, by the way.

01:20:57.760 --> 01:20:59.581
But I thought, that's fantastic.

01:20:59.921 --> 01:21:00.661
There's a,

01:21:03.654 --> 01:21:11.240
it's, on the other hand, so it's not the social status, it's the salary that, right?

01:21:11.961 --> 01:21:17.987
We were paid, yeah, obviously made good salary.

01:21:18.547 --> 01:21:21.430
So people combined based on their,

01:21:23.010 --> 01:21:25.855
financial status rather than social status.

01:21:26.415 --> 01:21:30.281
But I kind of like that.

01:21:30.642 --> 01:21:35.169
Now that you point that out, I guess that has a lot to do with the government maybe?

01:21:36.044 --> 01:21:38.485
Well, the history, I think.

01:21:38.565 --> 01:21:48.069
I mean, France, Paris was, when I was there, 2,000 years old, and now it's another 200 years.

01:21:50.510 --> 01:22:00.055
There's a whole, yeah, most of Western Europe, England included, there's this social thing.

01:22:01.515 --> 01:22:16.022
which is another thing that ham radio helps, because during those meetings, there was no, you didn't talk about, you know, I'm, you know, Dr. So-and-so, which was ham radio.

01:22:16.122 --> 01:22:17.843
So that was true in France, too.

01:22:17.963 --> 01:22:23.986
But now there were a lot of, and the opportunities later on, it,

01:22:30.898 --> 01:22:48.667
Yeah, in fact, of course now it wouldn't work either, but I was going to say, in France, without a PhD, you can't be teaching at the university, period.

01:22:50.190 --> 01:23:08.604
Here, that's true now, but because engineering technology was not preparing people for grad school, they say, okay, you don't have a PhD, but you've got 10 years of industrial experience, so for us, that's actually good.

01:23:09.365 --> 01:23:11.206
They no longer do that.

01:23:11.467 --> 01:23:12.347
In fact, when I...

01:23:14.049 --> 01:23:22.515
One year I made the outstanding prof for, you know, the College of Engineering.

01:23:22.595 --> 01:23:26.418
Each college has one, and I did it for the College of Engineering.

01:23:26.799 --> 01:23:31.402
So big receptions and speeches.

01:23:32.984 --> 01:23:39.228
And I was, so the ten of us are sitting together on the podium.

01:23:40.928 --> 01:23:57.160
And I hear the lady at that time who was in charge of the faculty, she was reading some letters from my students and compliments and very nice.

01:23:58.081 --> 01:24:03.184
But I was, so that's 19, I'm sorry, 2000, yeah.

01:24:03.244 --> 01:24:03.364
But

01:24:10.770 --> 01:24:16.452
If I applied now, you wouldn't even look at my resume because I don't have a doctorate, right?

01:24:16.932 --> 01:24:22.453
So it's kind of ironic, but in France there is no exception.

01:24:23.293 --> 01:24:25.534
You can't teach at the university if you don't have a PhD.

01:24:25.554 --> 01:24:27.354
Interesting.

01:24:27.594 --> 01:24:28.655
So that was a plus.

01:24:28.855 --> 01:24:38.577
And even, well, the junior college they wouldn't require, but the technical school in France, no, there you don't need a PhD either.

01:24:40.468 --> 01:24:55.218
So, there's definitely more opportunities here than to think.

01:24:55.558 --> 01:25:02.363
Things have changed in Europe a lot, but in that time, it was much more structured.

01:25:03.984 --> 01:25:09.728
Yeah, that makes sense.

01:25:11.657 --> 01:25:17.320
This life here is very comfortable.

01:25:17.380 --> 01:25:25.005
Lots of, what's the right word?

01:25:25.925 --> 01:25:29.187
Lots of stuff you couldn't do elsewhere, I think.

01:25:30.826 --> 01:25:33.406
It's a bit frenetic.

01:25:33.467 --> 01:25:38.147
There is something in France and the other Western European country.

01:25:38.647 --> 01:25:40.468
You have five weeks of vacation.

01:25:41.208 --> 01:25:43.368
Paid.

01:25:43.748 --> 01:25:47.449
So you've got a lot more time to spend with your kids.

01:25:49.389 --> 01:25:52.310
That I missed.

01:25:53.050 --> 01:25:53.910
And you could travel.

01:25:53.950 --> 01:25:57.871
I mean, I used to travel with my parents until I was...

01:26:00.200 --> 01:26:11.763
21 or something like that, that we would still go together because there's no perfect place.

01:26:12.463 --> 01:26:27.346
But I'm certainly grateful to this one, which is why I joined the Coast Guard Auxiliary, which is the volunteer branch of the Coast Guard, and I just completed my 25th year with them.

01:26:28.786 --> 01:26:34.051
That was my way of saying thanks, and here, maybe I can help.

01:26:34.291 --> 01:26:40.677
Yeah, I got my fourth gold star.

01:26:41.858 --> 01:26:47.222
The ribbon is the first five years, then they're each gold star is another five years.

01:26:47.963 --> 01:26:48.904
Yeah, that, yeah.

01:26:54.006 --> 01:26:54.967
So I still do that.

01:26:55.007 --> 01:27:11.635
Of course, I don't go on patrols anymore, boat patrols anymore, and I don't do physical stuff because I don't have the strength, and I have usual age problems.

01:27:12.896 --> 01:27:19.340
But I'm still a member, I'm still their communication officer for some strange reason.

01:27:21.661 --> 01:27:28.584
I was the HR officer for the division level.

01:27:28.604 --> 01:27:33.106
I stepped down from that last year because I was forgetting too many things.

01:27:33.126 --> 01:27:39.289
It's embarrassing when you're in HR, you deal with all the newcomers, right?

01:27:39.329 --> 01:27:42.190
Then you help, you have to do this and this.

01:27:42.870 --> 01:27:46.492
And then three weeks later, the guy would come back and...

01:27:48.446 --> 01:27:49.128
Do I know you?

01:27:49.268 --> 01:27:50.753
I mean, that's normal at my age.

01:27:56.276 --> 01:28:15.741
Yeah, in fact, we're going to have, we call that the flotilla, it's on Lake Conroe, and we're going to have a common maneuver training the end of May, May 30th.

01:28:16.701 --> 01:28:25.683
Together with the regular Coast Guard, the fire department, they have several rescue and fire boats.

01:28:26.443 --> 01:28:33.307
And the Lake Constable, we do a common maneuver, which is part of training.

01:28:33.787 --> 01:28:36.088
We do search and rescue type thing.

01:28:37.869 --> 01:28:43.993
Normally it's done on Galveston Bay, but it's...

01:28:45.783 --> 01:28:48.164
And we, yeah, we're the volunteer branch.

01:28:48.244 --> 01:28:52.825
We don't do law enforcement, but everything else.

01:28:55.186 --> 01:29:01.908
Public education, boat inspection, we do public affairs.

01:29:04.850 --> 01:29:15.094
radio communications when needed, and essentially whatever the regular Coast Guard would like us to do.

01:29:16.995 --> 01:29:24.719
It goes all the way to cooks, I mean chefs that were chefs in their previous life and are retired.

01:29:25.659 --> 01:29:29.881
And in fact that started in our division when they

01:29:34.364 --> 01:29:42.530
I don't think it was the captain, but one of the officers of the regular ghost girl said, wait a minute, do you have people who know how to cook?

01:29:43.250 --> 01:29:44.772
Yeah, we need them.

01:29:44.852 --> 01:29:45.832
We don't have enough.

01:29:45.872 --> 01:29:51.717
We have boats that cannot go out on shore because the only thing we have are sandwiches.

01:29:53.306 --> 01:30:10.089
And so there we have people that go help them at Galveston, but also fewer of them because you now have to go spend a week or two offshore with them.

01:30:15.136 --> 01:30:24.349
So we also have people who help them, administratively speaking, because they are so busy these days.

01:30:27.113 --> 01:30:39.065
both from a protection of the coast, that's a job, right, but blocking and you name it.

01:30:39.586 --> 01:30:45.011
And that's the role that they used to have, which included

01:30:46.072 --> 01:30:48.574
boating safety of the public, right?

01:30:50.476 --> 01:30:53.318
They say, look, we really have less and less time.

01:30:53.338 --> 01:30:54.659
You guys do that.

01:30:54.899 --> 01:31:12.953
So that's why we do patrol in Galveston Bay regularly and we have that on Lake Conroe and many of the lakes to reduce the number of accidents and deaths on the water.

01:31:14.918 --> 01:31:20.525
So since you've gone all over, moved everywhere, do you have anything else on your bucket list?

01:31:20.645 --> 01:31:22.307
Any places you want to visit?

01:31:23.208 --> 01:31:25.731
Well, I've been to quite a few places.

01:31:27.613 --> 01:31:28.394
That's the thing.

01:31:28.514 --> 01:31:32.899
You know, I mentioned the five-week vacation with my parents.

01:31:33.119 --> 01:31:34.040
We could go

01:31:36.179 --> 01:31:37.980
all over Western Europe.

01:31:38.780 --> 01:31:51.268
I did go to Eastern Europe to what was then Czechoslovakia as part of my job that first year for just a couple of weeks.

01:31:53.410 --> 01:31:56.172
But Prague is a very nice city.

01:31:57.432 --> 01:32:02.733
And my wife is actually from East Germany.

01:32:02.913 --> 01:32:07.414
They escaped just before the wall was completely closed.

01:32:08.334 --> 01:32:21.177
But of course, now it's recombined, so we went together with her brother, visiting where they were born.

01:32:22.338 --> 01:32:23.802
And that was interesting.

01:32:24.163 --> 01:32:26.809
In fact, the mayor somehow

01:32:29.949 --> 01:32:44.415
I guess her brother just went to the city hall to say, we're here for a visit, and we used to, I guess the mayor got interested and rejoined us and took us around.

01:32:44.435 --> 01:32:45.696
It was really nice.

01:32:49.417 --> 01:32:51.958
Yeah, I've been to lots of places.

01:32:53.519 --> 01:32:53.999
Also,

01:32:55.680 --> 01:33:06.183
I was a part-time contractor with USAID, which doesn't exist anymore.

01:33:06.443 --> 01:33:11.344
One department of USAID was the Office of Foreign Disaster Assistance.

01:33:11.964 --> 01:33:18.526
They go and help people right after, you know, hurricanes, volcanic eruption.

01:33:19.226 --> 01:33:21.988
to do help wherever needed.

01:33:22.708 --> 01:33:30.612
And of course, one problem, especially the smaller Caribbean islands, is they get wiped out.

01:33:31.213 --> 01:33:34.495
And they have no way to call for help.

01:33:35.655 --> 01:33:38.277
Well, dependent situation.

01:33:38.797 --> 01:33:45.601
So one of the things I did with another ham who was working for them full time,

01:33:46.281 --> 01:34:12.198
is we would go down and do a temporary either shortwave radio communication or we had a portable satellite station and set it up so they can talk either to Geneva, the Red Cross, or to Washington for the health organization.

01:34:12.718 --> 01:34:14.361
Doctors Without Borders?

01:34:14.401 --> 01:34:15.724
No, no, I mean the government.

01:34:17.046 --> 01:34:22.936
Well, it's international, not international, multinational power.

01:34:30.862 --> 01:34:37.567
The aid show is health organization, oh, Pan American Health Organization.

01:34:38.808 --> 01:34:45.853
For example, sometimes they would ask USAID, can you send us some guys?

01:34:47.054 --> 01:34:50.077
So, went to Guatemala and do that.

01:34:54.453 --> 01:34:56.675
They didn't do much others.

01:34:56.795 --> 01:35:02.180
Well, Mexico, yeah, but did sailing to Mexico several times.

01:35:02.900 --> 01:35:08.826
Alpanama on the Atlantic side.

01:35:12.521 --> 01:35:17.166
And yeah, a lot of Western, essentially most of Western Europe.

01:35:17.867 --> 01:35:23.994
I have lots of cousins, distant cousins.

01:35:25.155 --> 01:35:29.220
We had one major family, greater family reunion in

01:35:32.495 --> 01:35:41.553
Yeah, in November of 2000, first year I was retired, organized by people over there.

01:35:43.498 --> 01:35:55.703
And we had about 110 or 114 people from nine different countries, all descended from the same great-great-grandfather.

01:35:56.584 --> 01:36:02.106
And we were in that—it took place in the town where those grandfathers had lived.

01:36:03.828 --> 01:36:14.622
And, I mean, there was, we had people, yeah, including a family from Hungary.

01:36:15.543 --> 01:36:17.126
And at that time, in 2000, there was

01:36:19.409 --> 01:36:20.731
free traveling again.

01:36:20.791 --> 01:36:22.773
They had to go through all kinds of things.

01:36:22.793 --> 01:36:27.780
They drove all the way down to the east of France to join us.

01:36:27.800 --> 01:36:31.145
That's where my family was from.

01:36:31.165 --> 01:36:31.526
Yeah.

01:36:32.307 --> 01:36:34.710
And so we had people from Italy,

01:36:36.071 --> 01:36:59.323
Interestingly enough, we, nobody from Spain, yet Catala is definitely, it's from the northern, northeastern part, the place called Catalonia, in fact, which is a province, that in their language, Catalan, Catala means somebody from Catalonia, so we know we have ancestors there, but we couldn't go that far.

01:37:00.023 --> 01:37:12.277
So it was strange that we had people from Argentina, we had people from England, Germany, Italy, France of course.

01:37:14.700 --> 01:37:19.185
And I visited a lot of these people because

01:37:20.406 --> 01:37:31.450
We, that group that organized this reunion, then two years later, well, we go to another country where we're, and by that time I was part of the committee.

01:37:31.550 --> 01:37:37.313
So, yeah, I, yeah, traveling I did, a lot.

01:37:39.759 --> 01:37:54.279
In Canada, after I was here, one of our daughters lived in Washington State, almost in Canada, on the Pacific Ocean.

01:37:55.801 --> 01:38:13.349
So we have a camper van and we, several times, three times we've gone there, took our time going and spent a month there and come back at different routes and in fact, through Canada.

01:38:13.369 --> 01:38:13.669
Yeah.

01:38:20.275 --> 01:38:43.830
let's see, of course Haiti, Jamaica, and while I was there, so I was working for the International Telecommunication Union, but the United Nations,

01:38:51.407 --> 01:38:52.468
I'm sorry, I'm going to blame.

01:38:52.828 --> 01:39:00.632
Anyhow, they also, that's the specialized agency, the UN, that deals with disasters.

01:39:01.973 --> 01:39:13.699
And when I was in Haiti, usually all the project managers would get together once every two months or so to exchange ideas.

01:39:14.179 --> 01:39:16.861
And we had a representative from...

01:39:20.708 --> 01:39:23.229
And he said, oh, you're in telecom.

01:39:23.309 --> 01:39:25.489
We need help in the Caribbean with us.

01:39:25.509 --> 01:39:28.150
I said, okay.

01:39:28.410 --> 01:39:30.231
I've got a full-time job here.

01:39:30.251 --> 01:39:32.452
I said, Tim, who's your boss?

01:39:33.632 --> 01:39:34.272
Geneva.

01:39:34.632 --> 01:39:36.993
I said, oh, we're in Geneva, too.

01:39:41.134 --> 01:39:50.716
They organized something and I was allowed to, you know, take, I don't remember, four days off a month to go.

01:39:51.316 --> 01:40:02.139
So, and I visited the different islands, all the independent ones, not Puerto Rico and, you know, the ones that are still...

01:40:02.739 --> 01:40:08.302
territories or whatever, they have their civil defense organized.

01:40:08.763 --> 01:40:10.564
But the others need help.

01:40:11.524 --> 01:40:21.350
So I would stay several days in this island and another month in that island, which, yeah, I know the Caribbean fairly well.

01:40:21.750 --> 01:40:23.191
Wow, what a life.

01:40:23.551 --> 01:40:25.572
You've lived so much.

01:40:25.612 --> 01:40:26.693
You've seen so much.

01:40:27.833 --> 01:40:29.935
Yeah, I have been...

01:40:31.259 --> 01:40:33.980
Ever since the war stopped, I've been very lucky.

01:40:35.620 --> 01:40:36.160
Interesting.

01:40:37.700 --> 01:40:45.602
I know you mentioned how you were writing some chapters to a book, but I think you should write your own book about places you've been to.

01:40:46.322 --> 01:40:50.283
I'd for sure read that.

01:40:50.523 --> 01:40:51.303
Yeah.

01:40:54.484 --> 01:40:55.804
I don't have any more questions.

01:40:56.024 --> 01:40:59.905
Did you have anything that you wanted to talk about that I didn't get to?

01:41:03.288 --> 01:41:07.392
Yeah, a couple of things.

01:41:08.553 --> 01:41:15.000
One thing when I did decide to come here is when I thought, oh good, I'm gonna

01:41:17.702 --> 01:41:21.845
stop all the political problems.

01:41:23.346 --> 01:41:30.871
You work for the UN and in a foreign country, you've got to deal with a lot of different things.

01:41:31.091 --> 01:41:35.134
First, you've got colleagues that are from five different countries.

01:41:35.234 --> 01:41:41.779
We all have our own views of things, and those are the five experts.

01:41:43.800 --> 01:42:05.455
and then you've got our counterparts and of course they the whole country right and there are things that are perfectly okay the u.s or but not at all okay they say and and you don't have time to learn all you learn the hard way so it

01:42:13.153 --> 01:42:18.616
I mean, again, I'm not regretting any of that.

01:42:18.996 --> 01:42:23.099
But it's different.

01:42:23.359 --> 01:42:26.861
It's different.

01:42:27.461 --> 01:42:32.264
Haiti, in particular, is a very poor country.

01:42:33.773 --> 01:42:40.279
And they have a former French colony and then the history of slavery.

01:42:41.600 --> 01:42:44.342
And there are things that you just don't do.

01:42:45.443 --> 01:42:59.574
So if you ask something from somebody in a way that can be answered by yes or no, they can't say no.

01:42:59.594 --> 01:43:00.375
Oh.

01:43:01.252 --> 01:43:04.974
You can't say no to a superior, right?

01:43:05.935 --> 01:43:13.158
And especially a foreigner, a blanc, yeah, white guy.

01:43:14.099 --> 01:43:15.600
So they say yes.

01:43:17.661 --> 01:43:21.063
And then you say, you've finished that.

01:43:21.603 --> 01:43:23.504
I attested it's not working.

01:43:23.524 --> 01:43:26.125
I said, no, I don't know how to do that.

01:43:27.386 --> 01:43:29.067
But I ask you if you've done it.

01:43:30.268 --> 01:43:30.568
Yes.

01:43:32.618 --> 01:43:34.258
And I'm like, what's going on?

01:43:34.318 --> 01:43:43.800
So I went to see my colleague, my counterpart, who was an engineer, and he said, well, no, it's not an acceptable answer in this.

01:43:44.120 --> 01:43:44.740
Oh, geez.

01:43:45.381 --> 01:43:47.021
So you've got to make sure you ask.

01:43:47.201 --> 01:43:53.062
In fact, one time, we were looking for a place for a microwave repeater.

01:43:53.142 --> 01:43:57.283
We were in countryside, jeep fording rivers, and...

01:43:58.705 --> 01:44:01.626
And on the way back, I got totally lost.

01:44:01.987 --> 01:44:07.889
There was a lady sitting in front of a hut, literally.

01:44:08.970 --> 01:44:13.152
And I asked her, you know, is this the way to Port-au-Prince?

01:44:14.152 --> 01:44:15.293
Guess what she answered?

01:44:15.973 --> 01:44:16.954
Yes, right?

01:44:17.454 --> 01:44:23.298
So I go there and we drive for hours, maybe not, but for a long time.

01:44:23.318 --> 01:44:26.920
I said, I don't reckon any of this.

01:44:27.600 --> 01:44:28.381
Turn around.

01:44:32.073 --> 01:44:47.299
He said, one of the, it wasn't my counterpart, but one of the Asian colleagues, he was in training, he was an installer, said, do you mind if I ask the questions?

01:44:47.339 --> 01:44:48.299
He said, please.

01:44:49.040 --> 01:44:53.161
So he stated in Creole, not in French, but I understood.

01:44:53.681 --> 01:44:56.803
He said, which is the way for Port-au-Prince?

01:44:57.363 --> 01:44:59.824
And then, that way, I thought.

01:45:03.366 --> 01:45:05.427
Well, yeah, I should have...

01:45:07.689 --> 01:45:17.115
Ideally, you have an advance notice, you've got a month to read about, but that's not the way things happen.

01:45:17.235 --> 01:45:22.978
I had one week and a half to make a decision whether I would go to Haiti or not.

01:45:28.418 --> 01:45:30.901
Yeah, that was interesting.

01:45:31.741 --> 01:45:35.084
Yeah, cultural differences, yeah.

01:45:35.124 --> 01:45:37.767
See, I wouldn't even think about that.

01:45:37.907 --> 01:45:43.472
I would have that problem as well.

01:45:43.532 --> 01:45:47.796
Yeah, it's their history, and they still suffer from it.

01:45:48.376 --> 01:45:51.539
And actually, the other problem is

01:45:54.908 --> 01:46:05.798
even within a telephone company, the position that you occupy or you occupy is

01:46:08.709 --> 01:46:10.330
really a political assignment.

01:46:10.650 --> 01:46:11.551
Okay.

01:46:12.311 --> 01:46:15.694
So, at least at the higher level.

01:46:16.154 --> 01:46:23.899
So you have situation where a supervisor, and I pointed out, said, did you see what he did?

01:46:23.959 --> 01:46:24.239
Yeah.

01:46:25.540 --> 01:46:26.360
That's dangerous.

01:46:26.901 --> 01:46:29.662
Yes, but I don't want to give him a hard time.

01:46:31.103 --> 01:46:37.048
Because two months from now, maybe the doc might put him as my boss's boss.

01:46:40.211 --> 01:46:41.832
So can I tell him?

01:46:42.252 --> 01:46:43.173
Yes, please do.

01:46:44.674 --> 01:46:49.799
So even in those instances, you still have to play politics?

01:46:51.028 --> 01:46:54.611
Yeah, well, so yeah, you're right, I diverged.

01:46:55.231 --> 01:47:00.475
So I thought, I'm through with all this political... No.

01:47:04.578 --> 01:47:05.779
That was a surprise.

01:47:06.839 --> 01:47:11.923
Although I've been very lucky with all my department heads.

01:47:12.063 --> 01:47:18.228
Some interesting times.

01:47:18.248 --> 01:47:18.648
Yeah.

01:47:20.211 --> 01:47:39.499
Again, if I had known that I wouldn't have any vacation, I probably would have said no, because I had an offer from industry in San Diego, and of course, but that's the way it is.

01:47:40.519 --> 01:47:44.380
I wouldn't have a chance to have this relationship with the students, so.

01:47:46.875 --> 01:47:51.538
Thank you for meeting me and telling me your story, a bit of your story.

01:47:51.638 --> 01:47:55.760
I'm glad if it could be useful.

01:47:55.980 --> 01:47:56.861
Absolutely, it is.

01:47:57.281 --> 01:47:57.841
Absolutely.

01:47:59.903 --> 01:48:11.229
We have roughly 2,000 interviews from the 60s all the way up to the early 2000s from previous history.

01:48:11.389 --> 01:48:14.351
One of them was the director of our library, Cushing.

01:48:14.985 --> 01:48:17.207
And the other one, Terry Anderson, he's still here.

01:48:17.287 --> 01:48:18.628
He's a history professor.

01:48:18.648 --> 01:48:19.448
I don't know if you know him.

01:48:19.749 --> 01:48:22.050
I know the school's huge, but he's still here.

01:48:22.170 --> 01:48:32.238
And so once I got hired and I basically got all of his work that he had, I was like, it's so nice to meet you.

01:48:32.358 --> 01:48:36.321
And I'm the one handling all of your interviews that you've given me.

01:48:36.341 --> 01:48:38.602
He's like, oh, no, those things, put them back in the closet.

01:48:39.043 --> 01:48:42.405
Put them back in the closet.

01:48:42.725 --> 01:48:43.006
Yeah.

01:48:43.126 --> 01:48:44.086
Oh, good.

01:48:44.427 --> 01:48:44.687
Yeah.

01:48:45.546 --> 01:48:46.367
Thank you so much.

01:48:46.787 --> 01:48:47.828
You're very welcome.

01:48:48.588 --> 01:48:49.429
Glad to help.

01:48:49.589 --> 01:48:51.510
Yes.

01:48:51.530 --> 01:48:52.431
I will be in touch.

01:48:52.631 --> 01:48:55.133
I will make sure you get copies of everything.

01:48:59.155 --> 01:49:01.497
Now, your wife sounds like someone I should talk to, too.

01:49:01.557 --> 01:49:03.018
She is quite a lady.

01:49:03.098 --> 01:49:05.740
She's kept me for 57 years.

01:49:05.820 --> 01:49:10.783
That by itself, she's the nurse, was a nurse.

01:49:16.463 --> 01:49:19.066
after we came back from Haiti.

01:49:21.729 --> 01:49:31.782
She had been interested in medical stuff for a long time and did some of it over because the places were, you know, Haiti and...

01:49:33.123 --> 01:49:40.546
to a lesser extent Jamaica, where if you have to do something rapidly, there's no ambulance coming, right?

01:49:41.386 --> 01:49:51.290
So she read lots of books and things, and decided that she wanted to—we came back in, well, 1984.

01:49:53.591 --> 01:49:57.753
She couldn't really become a doctor, but she decided, I'm going to be a nurse.

01:49:58.373 --> 01:50:02.915
and I was here on campus so practically all the time.

01:50:03.475 --> 01:50:13.098
She managed with the four girls who were, well, twins were in senior year, and then Yvonne was

01:50:16.539 --> 01:50:17.519
two years behind.

01:50:18.019 --> 01:50:21.320
And then the little one, we adopted a baby in Haiti.

01:50:23.540 --> 01:50:40.124
So, schoolgirls, and she was studying for nursing, taking classes in the evening, you know, as we say in French, hats off.

01:50:40.804 --> 01:50:42.465
It's an amazing lady.

01:50:42.965 --> 01:50:43.045
And

01:50:45.486 --> 01:50:51.390
I mean, she had a rough youth too.

01:50:51.410 --> 01:50:58.174
The war was over, but I mean, East Germany was under, you know, communist thing.

01:50:59.795 --> 01:51:07.880
Yeah, her dad had a small factory and he had to escape.

01:51:09.380 --> 01:51:21.545
and her mother, her brother, and her escaped two years later, right before they completely closed the wall.

01:51:23.326 --> 01:51:27.227
And so they had to start their life all over in West Germany.

01:51:28.708 --> 01:51:31.169
Yeah, they had a rough time too.

01:51:31.989 --> 01:51:33.010
If she does want to talk.

01:51:34.729 --> 01:51:38.971
I would love to hear her story as well.

01:51:39.091 --> 01:51:39.831
I will ask.

01:51:39.851 --> 01:51:40.952
She probably not.

01:51:40.992 --> 01:51:50.716
She, for several reasons, doesn't like to talk about... That's understandable.

01:51:52.997 --> 01:51:58.320
But she's right now helping our...

